Title: TWO TICKETS
Credit: original spec pages
Author: audience-predictor
Draft date: 2026

FADE IN:

INT. GREYHOUND STATION - DAY

Rain on a high glass roof. Fluorescents the color of old milk. A board of departures ticks, misses a city, ticks again.

JULES (20s) stands at the counter with two printed tickets and a backpack that has been repaired with duct tape in three places.

CLERK
Chicago's full. I can do Toledo and a prayer.

JULES
I have two seats that exist.

CLERK
Then you should sit in them.

Jules turns. The only open pair is next to a stranger.

INT. BUS - DAY

ROW 14. Jules sits. Then stands. Then sits. Beside them: REN (20s), headphones, a paperback with no cover, orange peels in a napkin.

The bus lurches. Jules' coffee describes a small tragedy on Ren's paperback.

JULES
I can buy you a new one. I can't buy you the notes in the margin.

REN
Those were wrong anyway.

A beat. The wipers find a tempo.

REN
Toledo?

JULES
Chicago. Eventually. You?

REN
A wedding I was not invited to until Thursday.

JULES
Are you going to stop it or toast it?

REN
I packed for both.

EXT. REST STOP - DUSK

A vending machine that only believes in chips. Jules kicks it once, kindly. Two bags drop. They offer one. Ren takes it like a truce.

REN
If I miss this wedding, my sister will name a child after the insult.

JULES
If I miss Chicago, I miss a person who asked me to come and then stopped answering.

They eat chips in the rain like it is a plan.

INT. BUS - NIGHT

Most of the lights are off. The road is a rumor.

REN
I could get off at the next one. You could keep both tickets.

JULES
They're not both mine. One of them is a maybe.

REN
Maybes expire.

JULES
Not if you sit next to them.

Ren's hand, on the armrest, does not move away.

The board in Jules' head still says CHICAGO. The bus does not care.

FADE OUT.
